## Select Statement

`select` lets a goroutine **wait on multiple channel operations at once**. It's like a `switch`, but each case is a channel operation.

## Basic Syntax

```go
select {
case msg1 := <-ch1:
	fmt.Println("From ch1:", msg1)
case msg2 := <-ch2:
	fmt.Println("From ch2:", msg2)
}
```

If **multiple cases are ready simultaneously**, Go picks one **randomly**.

## Multi-Channel Example

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"time"
)

func main() {
	ch1 := make(chan string)
	ch2 := make(chan string)

	go func() {
		time.Sleep(1 * time.Second)
		ch1 <- "one"
	}()

	go func() {
		time.Sleep(2 * time.Second)
		ch2 <- "two"
	}()

	for i := 0; i < 2; i++ {
		select {
		case msg := <-ch1:
			fmt.Println("Received from ch1:", msg)
		case msg := <-ch2:
			fmt.Println("Received from ch2:", msg)
		}
	}
}
```

## Default Case (Non-Blocking)

The `default` case runs when **no channel is ready**:

```go
select {
case msg := <-ch:
	fmt.Println("received:", msg)
default:
	fmt.Println("no message ready") // non-blocking
}
```

## Timeout Pattern

Combine `select` with `time.After` for timeouts:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"time"
)

func slowOperation(ch chan<- string) {
	time.Sleep(3 * time.Second)
	ch <- "result"
}

func main() {
	ch := make(chan string)
	go slowOperation(ch)

	select {
	case result := <-ch:
		fmt.Println("Got:", result)
	case <-time.After(2 * time.Second):
		fmt.Println("Timeout! Operation took too long.")
	}
}
```

## Done Channel Pattern

```go
func worker(done <-chan struct{}) {
	for {
		select {
		case <-done:
			fmt.Println("worker stopping")
			return
		default:
			// do work
		}
	}
}
```

